Yes — you can absolutely build a **fully functional “jq-only SDK”** for Black Duck that feels almost like a real SDK, but consists of nothing more than a few `.jq` files and a tiny wrapper script.  
Support engineers love this because it’s zero dependencies beyond `jq` and `curl`, works on any Linux/macOS/Windows box (even air-gapped ones), and is completely version-controllable in Git.

Here’s a practical, battle-tested pattern that many Black Duck support teams already use in 2025.

### 1. Core Idea
Turn the Postman collection into a **self-documenting, executable jq library** that:
- Lets you call any Black Duck API with one line:  
  `bd projects list`  
  `bd scans upload my-project scan.xml`  
  `bd vulnerabilities critical my-project`
- Handles authentication, variables, pagination, and pretty output automatically
- Needs only `jq` ≥ 1.6 and `curl`

### 2. File Layout (5 files total)

```bash
blackduck-jq-sdk/
├── bd                # ← main executable wrapper (bash)
├── lib/
│   ├── auth.jq       # token handling
│   ├── request.jq    # generic HTTP caller
│   ├── collection.jq # parsed Postman collection (generated once)
│   └── helpers.jq    # pagination, error handling, pretty tables
└── README.md
```

### 3. Step-by-Step How to Build It

#### Step 1 – One-time: extract everything from the Postman collection

```bash
curl -s "https://your-bd-server/api-doc/postman-collection-public.json" \
  | jq '
    # Build a nice lookup table: name → request object
    [ .item[] 
      | . as $folder
      | .item[]
      | select(.request) 
      | {key: "\($folder.name | ascii_downcase | gsub(" ";""))_\(.name | ascii_downcase | gsub("[^a-z0-9]+";"_"))",
        value: .}
    ] | from_entries
  ' > lib/collection.jq
```

Now `lib/collection.jq` contains something you can query like:
```jq
include "collection"; 
projects_get_all_projects   # → the full request object
```

#### Step 2 – lib/request.jq (the heart of the SDK)

```jq
def request($method; $path; $body):
  # $bd_base and $bd_token are injected from environment by the wrapper
  env.bd_base as $base
  | env.bd_token as $token
  | [
      "curl", "-sS", "-X", $method,
      "\($base)\($path)",
      "-H", "Authorization: Bearer \($token)",
      "-H", "Accept: application/json"
    ]
    + (if $body then ["-H", "Content-Type: application/json", "-d", $body] else [] end)
    | @sh   # → returns ready-to-exec string
  ;

def get($path):    request("GET";    $path; null)
def post($path; $b): request("POST"; $path; $b)
def put($path; $b):  request("PUT";  $path; $b)
def delete($path): request("DELETE"; $path; null)
```

#### Step 3 – lib/helpers.jq (nice stuff)

```jq
def paginate:
  . as $raw
  | if .totalCount? and (.items | length) < .totalCount then
      .items,
      (_links.next.href | capture("/api/(?<p>.*)$").p | "/api/\(.)" | paginate)
    else .items end;

def table:
  [.[0] | keys_unsorted[]] as $headers
  | $headers,
    (.[] | [.[$headers[]]] | @tsv)
  | @tsv;
```

#### Step 4 – The tiny wrapper (bd)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

export bd_base="${BD_URL:-https://your-bd-server}"
export bd_token="${BD_TOKEN:-$(cat ~/.bd/token)}"

cmd="$1"; shift || { echo "Usage: bd <folder>_<name> [args...]"; exit 1; }

# Find the request object in the parsed collection
request_json=$(jq -rn \
  --arg cmd "$cmd" \
  'include "collection";
   include "request";
   include "helpers";
   $collection[$cmd]')

# If nothing found, nice error
if [ "$request_json" = "null" ]; then
  echo "Unknown command: $cmd"
  echo "Available commands:"
  jq -r 'include "collection"; $collection | keys[]' lib/collection.jq | sort
  exit 1
fi

# Execute the generated curl command and pipe through jq for pretty/errors
eval "$(echo "$request_json" | jq -r '
  .request as $r
  | get($r.url.raw)          # builds curl command
')"

# Add default pretty-print + pagination for GETs
if [[ $cmd == *"get"* ]] || [[ $cmd == *"list"* ]]; then
  jq -r 'include "helpers"; . | paginate | table // .'
else
  jq .
fi
```

Make it executable: `chmod +x bd`

### 4. Real Usage Examples (this is what support engineers actually type)

```bash
# Set once
export BD_URL=https://blackduck.company.com
